Not in your sources — from general knowledge: The most modern and recommended way to center a `div` horizontally is using **Flexbox**.

### Method 1: Flexbox (Recommended)
Apply these styles to the **parent** container of the div you want to center.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

*   `display: flex`: Turns the parent into a flex container.
*   `justify-content: center`: Aligns child elements along the main axis (horizontally by default).

---

### Method 2: Margin Auto (For block-level elements)
If your div has a defined width, you can use automatic margins on both sides. This works for standard block layout without needing Flexbox or Grid.

```css
.child {
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
  /* Or shorthand */
  margin: 0 auto; 
  width: 50%; /* Must have a specified width */
}
```

---

### Method 3: CSS Grid
Another modern approach using Grid on the parent container.

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* Centers horizontally and vertically */

  /* For horizontal only with Grid: */
  justify-content: center; 
}
```

### Summary
*   Use **Flexbox** (`justify-content: center`) for most cases—it’s flexible, handles multiple items well, and doesn’t require setting widths.
*   Use **Margin Auto** if you’re working with simple block layouts or need to support very old browsers (though Flexbox is supported everywhere now).
